window.docContents[27].resolve({"/Cliques/#section-finite-cliques":{"contents":"Examples of cliques\n\nIf s : Finset V is a clique with n vertices then we call it an n-clique. This\nis IsNClique in Lean, which is a structure that bundles a proof that s is a\nclique together with a proof that s has cardinality n.\n\nNotice that IsClique takes a Set V while IsNClique takes a Finset V. A clique\nmay be infinite, so IsClique works with sets. But an n-clique needs a size in\nℕ, and #s is a natural number for a finset (and computable, so decide and\n#eval can use it). The field isClique : G.IsClique s silently coerces the finset\ns to a set. This coercion is written ↑s (or (s : Set V)), and we will see it\nin many statements below, such as G.IsIndepSet ↑s.\n\n#source IsNClique -- structure IsNClique (n : ℕ) (s : Finset α) : Prop where\n                  --                 isClique : G.IsClique s\n                  --                 card_eq : #s = n\n\n\nThe Finset {a, b} is a 2-clique if and only if G.Adj a b holds.\n\nlemma is2Clique_pair_iff {a b : V} [DecidableEq V] :\n    G.IsNClique 2 {a, b} ↔ G.Adj a b := by\n  constructor <;> intro h\n  · apply h.isClique (by grind) (by grind)\n    rintro rfl\n    grind [h.card_eq]\n  · constructor\n    · simp [h]\n    · grind [h.ne]\n\n\nThe Finset {a, b, c} is a 3-clique if and only if a, b, and c are pairwise\nadjacent. Notice in this and the previous lemma we need to give Lean a\nDecidableEq V instance to be able to form the Finset {a, b} and {a, b, c}.\nAlso we don't need to assume that the vertices in the cliques are distinct because\npairwise adjacency trivially implies that they are distinct.\n\nexample {a b c : V} [DecidableEq V] :\n    G.IsNClique 3 {a, b, c} ↔ G.Adj a b ∧ G.Adj a c ∧ G.Adj b c :=\n  is3Clique_triple_iff\n\n\nIf we insert a vertex a into an n-clique s and a is adjacent to every vertex\nin s then insert a s is an (n + 1)-clique.\n\n#check IsNClique.insert\n\n\nFor concrete finite graphs decide can check small examples directly.\n\nexample : K[9].IsNClique 5 {0, 1, 3, 5, 7} := by decide\n\n\nFamously the Mycielskian of any triangle-free graph is also triangle-free, so in\nparticular any iterated Mycielskian of the 5-cycle is triangle-free. (We will prove\nthis and a more general result later.)\n\nFor now we just check that a particular set of 3 vertices in mμ^3 C[5] is not a\n3-clique.\n\nexample :\n    ¬ (mμ^3 C[5]).IsNClique 3 {(0 : Fin 5)ᵣᵣᵣ, (1 : Fin 5)ₗₗₗ, (2 : Fin 5)ₗᵣₗ} := by\n  decide\n\n\nA graph homomorphism G →g H is injective on any clique: distinct vertices of a\nclique are adjacent, so their images are adjacent, hence distinct (since H is\nloopless). This generalizes Hom.injective_of_top_hom (the case where the graph is a\nclique).\n\nlemma Hom.injOn_of_isClique {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} (f : G →g H)\n    {s : Set V} (hs : G.IsClique s) : Set.InjOn f s := by\n  intro x hx y hy hxy\n  by_contra hne\n  exact H.irrefl (hxy ▸ f.map_adj (hs hx hy hne))\n\n\nNext, if a map f : V → W is injective on an n-clique s of G and\nsends adjacent vertices of s to adjacent vertices of H then the image of s is\nan n-clique of H. (We will use this several times, with f a graph homomorphism\nor a projection.)\n\nlemma IsNClique.image_of_adj {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} [DecidableEq W]\n    {n : ℕ} {s : Finset V} (hs : G.IsNClique n s) {f : V → W} (hinj : Set.InjOn f s)\n    (hadj : ∀ ⦃x⦄, x ∈ s → ∀ ⦃y⦄, y ∈ s → G.Adj x y → H.Adj (f x) (f y)) :\n    H.IsNClique n (s.image f) := by\n  refine ⟨?_, by rw [Finset.card_image_of_injOn hinj, hs.card_eq]⟩\n  rintro _ ha _ hb hne\n  obtain ⟨x, hx, rfl⟩ := Finset.mem_image.mp ha\n  obtain ⟨y, hy, rfl⟩ := Finset.mem_image.mp hb\n  exact hadj hx hy (hs.isClique hx hy fun h => hne (by rw [h]))\n\n\nUsing Hom.injOn_of_isClique we can show that the image of an n-clique under a\ngraph homomorphism is an n-clique.\n\nprotected lemma IsNClique.mapHom {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} [DecidableEq W]\n    {n : ℕ} {s : Finset V} (hs : G.IsNClique n s) (f : G →g H) :\n    H.IsNClique n (s.image f) :=\n  hs.image_of_adj (f.injOn_of_isClique hs.isClique) fun _ _ _ _ h => f.map_adj h\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Cliques","header":"6.2. Finite Cliques","id":"/Cliques/#section-finite-cliques"},"/Colorings/#section-exercises-colorings":{"contents":"1. Show that the cycle graph C[5] is not 2-colourable.\n\nexample : ¬ C[5].Colorable 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n2. Show that if G has an edge then 2 ≤ χ G. Hint: the endpoints of an edge form\na clique, so you can use IsClique.card_le_chromaticNumber.\n\nexample {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) : 2 ≤ χ G := by\n  sorry\n\n\n3. Show that a graph G on n vertices satisfies n ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ . Hint: combine\nnatCard_le_chromaticNumber_mul_indepNum with cliqueNum_compl.\n\nexample : Nat.card V ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n4. Show that the chromatic number of the hypercube Q[n + 1] is 2. Hint:\nchromaticNumber_eq_two_iff reduces this to facts proved in this chapter.\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : χ Q[n + 1] = 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n5. The Kneser graph Kn[n, k] is (n - 2 * k + 2)-colourable for 2 * k ≤ n and\n0 < k. (Recall Kn[n, k] is the graph whose vertices are the k-element subsets\nof Fin n = {0, ..., n - 1} with edges between disjoint subsets. This is the easy\npart of the Kneser–Lovász theorem.) The colouring is given below: colour a vertex by\nits least element, merging all the large colours into one. Show that it is a proper\ncolouring. This is the hardest exercise in this chapter: the case where both\nvertices receive the merged colour needs a pigeonhole argument which is in Mathlib as\nFinset.inter_nonempty_of_card_lt_card_add_card.\n\n/-- The explicit (n - 2 * k + 2)-colouring of the Kneser graph Kn[n, k]. -/\ndef kneserColoring' (n k : ℕ) (hn : 2 * k ≤ n) (hk : 0 < k) :\n    Kn[n, k].Coloring (Fin (n - 2 * k + 2)) where\n  toFun s :=\n    -- A vertex is s : Finset (Fin n) of size k. Since 0 < k, s is\n    -- nonempty. We colour s by its minimum element m.\n    let m : Fin n := s.val.min' (card_pos.mp (by rw [s.property]; exact hk))\n    -- Minima below n - 2 * k + 1 keep their own colour; every larger minimum\n    -- shares the last colour n - 2 * k + 1.\n    if h : m.val < n - 2 * k + 1 then\n      ⟨m.val, by grind⟩\n    else\n      ⟨n - 2 * k + 1, by grind⟩\n  map_rel' := by\n    sorry\n\n\n6. Show that if 0 < k, 2 * k ≤ n and Kn[n, k] is m-colourable then\nn ≤ k * m, so that χ Kn[n, k] ≥ n / k. Hint: use\nColorable.natCard_le_mul_indepNum and the independence number of the Kneser graph,\nindepNum_kneserGraph, from the previous chapter; Nat.add_one_mul_choose_eq will\nhelp with the binomial coefficients.\n\ntheorem le_mul_of_colorable_kneserGraph' {n k m : ℕ} (hk : 0 < k) (hn : 2 * k ≤ n)\n    (h : Kn[n, k].Colorable m) : n ≤ k * m := by\n  sorry\n\n\n7. Use the previous two exercises to show that the Petersen graph Kn[5, 2] has\nchromatic number 3.\n\nexample : χ Kn[5, 2] = 3 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n8. Prove the claim from the chapter that for G : SimpleGraph ℕ a vertex v has at\nmost v earlier neighbours.\n\nlemma degreeLT_le_self' (G : SimpleGraph ℕ) (v : ℕ) [Fintype (G.neighborLTSet v)] :\n    G.degreeLT v ≤ v := by\n  sorry\n\n\n9. The infinite star starNat joins 0 to every other natural number. Since 0\nhas infinitely many neighbours, starNat is not locally finite, so the bound\ncolorable_of_degree_le_of_countable does not apply. Show that it is nevertheless\n2-colourable, using colorable_of_degreeLT_le: every vertex has at most one\nearlier neighbour.\n\n/-- The infinite star on ℕ: the vertex 0 is adjacent to every other vertex. -/\nabbrev starNat : SimpleGraph ℕ where\n  Adj a b := (a = 0 ∧ b ≠ 0) ∨ (b = 0 ∧ a ≠ 0)\n\n\nexample : (starNat.neighborSet 0).Infinite ∧ starNat.Colorable 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n10. Show that the greedy colouring of the complete graph on ℕ colours vertex n\nwith colour n. (See greedy_crownGraph_eq for a similar proof using\nNat.strong_induction_on.)\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : (⊤ : SimpleGraph ℕ).greedy n = n := by\n  sorry\n\n\n11. The two definitions of the encoded version of a graph G : SimpleGraph V with\n[Encodable V] as a SimpleGraph ℕ are the same.\n\nexample [Encodable V] {G : SimpleGraph V} : G.encode = G.encodeMap := by\n  sorry\n\n\n1. Show that the cycle graph C[5] is not 2-colourable.example : ¬ C[5].Colorable 2 := by\n  rw [← chromaticNumber_le_iff_colorable,\n    chromaticNumber_cycleGraph_of_odd 5 (by decide) (by decide)]\n  decide\n2. Show that if G has an edge then 2 ≤ χ G. Hint: the endpoints of an edge form\na clique, so you can use IsClique.card_le_chromaticNumber.example {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) : 2 ≤ χ G := by\n  classical\n  have hc : G.IsClique ({u, v} : Finset V) := by simpa using fun _ => h\n  simpa [card_pair h.ne] using hc.card_le_chromaticNumber\n3. Show that a graph G on n vertices satisfies n ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ. Hint: combine\nnatCard_le_chromaticNumber_mul_indepNum with cliqueNum_compl.example : Nat.card V ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ :=\n  -- an independent set of G is a clique of Gᶜ\n  calc (Nat.card V : ℕ∞) ≤ χ G * ⍺ G := natCard_le_chromaticNumber_mul_indepNum\n    _ = χ G * ω Gᶜ := by rw [cliqueNum_compl]\n    _ ≤ χ G * χ Gᶜ := by gcongr; exact cliqueNum_le_chromaticNumber\n4. Show that the chromatic number of the hypercube Q[n + 1] is 2. Hint:\nchromaticNumber_eq_two_iff reduces this to facts proved in this chapter.example (n : ℕ) : χ Q[n + 1] = 2 := by\n  -- bipartiteness was proved in this chapter, so it only remains to show\n  -- Q[n + 1] ≠ ⊥ for which we need a vertex of Q[n] (each gives an edge in\n  -- the next dimension)\n  obtain ⟨v⟩ : Nonempty (HypercubeVert n) := inferInstance\n  refine chromaticNumber_eq_two_iff.2 ⟨hypercube_isBipartite _,?_⟩\n  exact  ne_bot_iff_exists_adj.2 ⟨(v, false), (v, true), by simp⟩\n5. The Kneser graph Kn[n, k] is (n - 2 * k + 2)-colourable for 2 * k ≤ n and\n0 < k. (Recall Kn[n, k] is the graph whose vertices are the k-element subsets\nof Fin n = {0, ..., n - 1} with edges between disjoint subsets. This is the easy\npart of the Kneser–Lovász theorem.) The colouring is given below: colour a vertex by\nits least element, merging all the large colours into one. Show that it is a proper\ncolouring. This is the hardest exercise in this chapter: the case where both\nvertices receive the merged colour needs a pigeonhole argument which is in Mathlib as\nFinset.inter_nonempty_of_card_lt_card_add_card./-- The explicit (n - 2 * k + 2)-colouring of the Kneser graph Kn[n, k]. -/\ndef kneserColoring (n k : ℕ) (hn : 2 * k ≤ n) (hk : 0 < k) :\n    Kn[n, k].Coloring (Fin (n - 2 * k + 2)) where\n  toFun s :=\n    -- A vertex is s : Finset (Fin n) of size k. Since 0 < k, s is\n    -- nonempty. We colour s by its minimum element m.\n    let m : Fin n := s.val.min' (card_pos.mp (by rw [s.property]; exact hk))\n    -- Minima below n - 2 * k + 1 keep their own colour; every larger minimum\n    -- shares the last colour n - 2 * k + 1.\n    if h : m.val < n - 2 * k + 1 then\n      ⟨m.val, by grind⟩\n    else\n      ⟨n - 2 * k + 1, by grind⟩\n  map_rel' := by\n    intro u v hadj hcolor\n    simp only [kneserGraph_adj, ne_eq, Order.lt_add_one_iff] at hadj hcolor\n    split_ifs at hcolor with h1 h2 h3\n    · -- the colour is the common minimum of u and v, which are disjoint\n      have hune : u.val.Nonempty := card_pos.mp (by rw [u.2]; exact hk)\n      have hvne : v.val.Nonempty := card_pos.mp (by rw [v.2]; exact hk)\n      have h : u.val.min' hune = v.val.min' hvne :=\n        Fin.val_injective (by simpa using congrArg Fin.val hcolor)\n      exact Finset.disjoint_left.mp hadj.1 (min'_mem _ hune) (h ▸ min'_mem _ hvne)\n    · grind -- impossible since the equal colours are not equal\n    · grind -- impossible since the equal colours are not equal\n    · push Not at h1 h3\n      -- both u and v lie in {n - 2 * k + 1, ..., n - 1}, which has only\n      -- 2 * k - 1 elements, so by the pigeonhole principle they meet\n      let a : Fin n := ⟨n - 2 * k, by grind⟩\n      have hsub (s : Finset (Fin n)) (hne : s.Nonempty)\n          (hs : n - 2 * k < ↑(s.min' hne)) : s ⊆ Ioi a :=\n        fun x hx => mem_Ioi.2 (hs.trans_le (min'_le _ x hx))\n      refine not_disjoint_iff_nonempty_inter.2 ?_ hadj.1\n      exact inter_nonempty_of_card_lt_card_add_card (hsub _ _ h1) (hsub _ _ h3)\n        (by grind [Fin.card_Ioi, u.2, v.2])\n6. Show that if 0 < k, 2 * k ≤ n and Kn[n, k] is m-colourable then\nn ≤ k * m, so that χ Kn[n, k] ≥ n / k. Hint: use\nColorable.natCard_le_mul_indepNum and the independence number of the Kneser graph,\nindepNum_kneserGraph, from the previous chapter; Nat.add_one_mul_choose_eq will\nhelp with the binomial coefficients.theorem le_mul_of_colorable_kneserGraph {n k m : ℕ} (hk : 0 < k) (hn : 2 * k ≤ n)\n    (h : Kn[n, k].Colorable m) : n ≤ k * m := by\n  have : NeZero k := ⟨hk.ne'⟩\n  -- Kn[n, k] has n.choose k vertices and independence number\n  -- (n - 1).choose (k - 1)\n  have hcard := h.natCard_le_mul_indepNum\n  have hkn : k ≤ n / 2 := by grind\n  simp only [indepNum_kneserGraph, hkn, ↓reduceIte, Nat.card_eq_fintype_card,\n    Fintype.card_finset_len, Fintype.card_fin] at hcard\n  -- n * (n - 1).choose (k - 1) = n.choose k * k\n  have key := Nat.add_one_mul_choose_eq (n - 1) (k - 1)\n  rw [Nat.sub_add_cancel (by grind), Nat.sub_add_cancel hk] at key\n  have hpos : 0 < (n - 1).choose (k - 1) := Nat.choose_pos (by grind)\n  refine Nat.le_of_mul_le_mul_right ?_ hpos\n  calc n * (n - 1).choose (k - 1) = k * n.choose k := by rw [key, mul_comm]\n    _ ≤ k * (m * (n - 1).choose (k - 1)) := Nat.mul_le_mul_left k hcard\n    _ = k * m * (n - 1).choose (k - 1) := by ring\n7. Use the previous two exercises to show that the Petersen graph Kn[5, 2] has\nchromatic number 3.example : χ Kn[5, 2] = 3 :=\n  -- kneserColoring uses 5 - 2 * 2 + 2 = 3 colours, and 2 colours would need\n  -- 5 ≤ 2 * 2\n  chromaticNumber_eq_iff_colorable_not_colorable.2\n    ⟨by simpa using (kneserColoring 5 2 (by norm_num) (by norm_num)).colorable,\n     fun h => by\n       simpa using le_mul_of_colorable_kneserGraph (by norm_num) (by norm_num) h⟩\n8. Prove the claim from the chapter that for G : SimpleGraph ℕ a vertex v has at\nmost v earlier neighbours.lemma degreeLT_le_self (G : SimpleGraph ℕ) (v : ℕ) [Fintype (G.neighborLTSet v)] :\n    G.degreeLT v ≤ v := by\n  -- the earlier neighbours of v lie in {0, 1, …, v - 1}\n  nth_rw 2 [← Finset.card_range v]\n  apply Finset.card_le_card\n  intro x hx; simp_all\n9. The infinite star starNat joins 0 to every other natural number. Since 0\nhas infinitely many neighbours, starNat is not locally finite, so the bound\ncolorable_of_degree_le_of_countable does not apply. Show that it is nevertheless\n2-colourable, using colorable_of_degreeLT_le.example : (starNat.neighborSet 0).Infinite ∧ starNat.Colorable 2 := by\n  refine ⟨Set.infinite_of_injective_forall_mem (f := Nat.succ) Nat.succ_injective\n    (by simp), starNat.colorable_of_degreeLT_le (Δ := 1) fun v => ?_⟩\n  -- the only earlier neighbour that a vertex can have is 0\n  rw [degreeLT, Finset.card_le_one]\n  intro a ha b hb\n  rw [mem_neighborLTFinset] at ha hb\n  grind\n10. Show that the greedy colouring of the complete graph on ℕ colours vertex n\nwith colour n. (See greedy_crownGraph_eq for a similar proof using\nNat.strong_induction_on.)example (n : ℕ) : (⊤ : SimpleGraph ℕ).greedy n = n := by\n  -- as in greedy_crownGraph_eq we use strong induction on n\n  induction n using Nat.strong_induction_on with\n  | _ n ih =>\n  -- the earlier neighbours of n are {0, ..., n - 1}, and by induction they use\n  -- exactly the colours {0, ..., n - 1}\n  refine (⊤ : SimpleGraph ℕ).greedy_eq_of_image_eq_range ?_\n  ext c\n  grind [mem_neighborLTFinset, top_adj]\n11. The two definitions of the encoded version of a graph G : SimpleGraph V with\n[Encodable V] as a SimpleGraph ℕ are the same.example [Encodable V] {G : SimpleGraph V} : G.encode = G.encodeMap := by\n  ext a b\n  simp only [encode_adj, encodeMap_adj]\n  constructor\n  · intro h\n    match hda : Encodable.decode₂ V a, hdb : Encodable.decode₂ V b with\n    | some u, some v =>\n        simp only [hda, hdb] at h\n        rw [Encodable.decode₂_eq_some] at hda hdb\n        have hab : a ≠ b := by\n          rintro rfl\n          rw [← hdb, Encodable.encode_inj] at hda\n          exact h.ne hda\n        exact ⟨hab, u, v, h, hda, hdb⟩\n    | none, _ | _, none => grind\n  · rintro ⟨h, u, v, hadj, rfl, rfl⟩\n    simpa\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings","header":"7.9. Exercises","id":"/Colorings/#section-exercises-colorings"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-order":{"contents":"There is a partial order on SimpleGraph V given by G ≤ H iff G is a subgraph of\nH. Note that these are necessarily spanning subgraphs since all\nSimpleGraph V have the same vertex set. We will consider general subgraphs later,\nsee Subgraphs.\n\nThis gives SimpleGraph V the structure of a complete atomic Boolean algebra, with\noperations sup, inf, compl and sdiff, and elements top and bot. This\nterminology comes from order theory rather than graph theory, but it is very natural\nin the context of formalization. Here complete means that every set of graphs in\nSimpleGraph V has a supremum (the graph whose edges are those present in at least\none graph in the set) and an infimum (the graph whose edges are those present in\nevery graph in the set). The atoms of SimpleGraph V are the graphs with exactly\none edge, and atomic means that every graph is the supremum of the atoms below it,\ni.e. the union of its single-edge subgraphs.\n\n#synth CompleteAtomicBooleanAlgebra (SimpleGraph V)\n\n\n#source SimpleGraph.instLE -- le x y := ∀ ⦃v w : V⦄, x.Adj v w → y.Adj v w\n\n\nvariable (H : SimpleGraph V)\n#check G ≤ H\n\n\nThe definition of proper subgraph (G < H) is phrased differently to how we might\nexpect. Rather than G < H ↔ G ≤ H ∧ G ≠ H we have:\n\nexample : G < H ↔ G ≤ H ∧ ¬ H ≤ G := by rfl\n\n\nOf course they are equivalent.\n\nexample : G < H ↔ G ≤ H ∧ G ≠ H := lt_iff_le_and_ne\n\n\nThe sup of G and H, denoted G ⊔ H, is the SimpleGraph V in which any pair\nof vertices from V are adjacent iff they are adjacent in G or H (i.e. it is the\nunion of G and H).\n\n#check sup_adj\n\n\nThe inf of G and H, denoted G ⊓ H, is the SimpleGraph V in which any pair\nof vertices from V are adjacent iff they are adjacent in both G and H (i.e. it\nis the intersection of G and H).\n\nThe inf and sup of two graphs\n\n#check inf_adj\n\n\nThe complement of G, denoted Gᶜ, is the SimpleGraph V in which the edges are\nall pairs of distinct vertices that are not adjacent in G.\n\n#check compl_adj\n\n\nThe set difference of two graphs, denoted G \\ H, has the edges of G with the\nedges of H removed. (Since the resulting graph is a SimpleGraph V the vertices\nare necessarily unchanged.)\n\n#check sdiff_adj\n\n\nThe complement and set difference\n\nWe can remove a set of edges s : Set (Sym2 V) from a graph G to form\nG.deleteEdges s. If s = H.edgeSet then this is the same as the set-difference\nG \\ H. (As ever, vertices are unchanged.)\n\n#source deleteEdges\n#check deleteEdges_adj      -- (G.deleteEdges s).Adj v w ↔ G.Adj v w ∧ s(v, w) ∉ s\n#check deleteEdges_edgeSet  -- G.deleteEdges H.edgeSet = G \\ H\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph","header":"2.5. Order","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-order"},"/Walks/#section-circuits-and-cycles":{"contents":"A circuit is a closed trail p (i.e. a walk from a vertex to itself without\nrepeated edges) that is not nil.\n\n#source Walk.IsCircuit -- Prop extends isTrail : IsTrail p where\n                       --               ne_nil : p ≠ nil\n\n\nA cycle is defined in Mathlib as a circuit in which the tail of its support has\nno repeated vertices.\n\n#source Walk.IsCycle  -- Prop extends isCircuit : IsCircuit p where\n                      --          support_nodup : p.support.tail.Nodup\n\n\nA circuit that is not a cycle, and a cycle, in the same graph\n\nAny cycle is a circuit.\n\nexample {u} (c : G.Walk u u) (hc : c.IsCycle) : c.IsCircuit := hc.isCircuit\n\n\nSince a cycle is a circuit, it is not equal to nil.\n\nexample {u} (c : G.Walk u u) (hc : c.IsCycle) : c ≠ nil := hc.isCircuit.ne_nil\n\n\nThere is a 3-cycle in the complete graph K[4].\n\nexample : ∃ (c₃ : K[4].Walk 0 0), c₃.IsCycle ∧ c₃.length = 3 := by\n  let c₃: K[4].Walk 0 0 := cons' 0 1 _ (by decide) <|\n            cons (by decide) <| cons' 2 0 _ (by decide) nil\n  exact ⟨c₃, by simp [c₃, isCycle_def, isTrail_def]⟩\n\n\nThe cycle c₃ defined above and pictured below is cons applied three times: it\nfollows the edges 0 — 1, 1 — 2 and 2 — 0, so it is the cycle 0 1 2 0. The\nremaining vertex 3 of K[4] is not used.\n\nThe 3-cycle 0 1 2 0 in the complete graph K_4\n\nA closed walk is a cycle if and only if it has length at least 3 and its tail is a\npath.\n\n#check isCycle_iff_isPath_tail_and_le_length\n\n\nIf c is a cycle then getVert is injective on {i | 1 ≤ i ≤ c.length}.\n\n#check IsCycle.getVert_injOn\n\n\nSo when is a cons h p walk a cycle? Clearly we need p to be a path, and also h\nmust be the adjacency from the end of p to the start of p (otherwise the new walk\nis not closed). Given these two hypotheses, how could cons h p fail to be a cycle?\nWell, we certainly have (cons h p).support.tail.Nodup since this is just\np.support.Nodup and p is a path. Also a cons walk is never nil. So the\nfailure must be that while p is a trail, cons h p is not a trail. This can only\nhappen if the edge corresponding to h is already present. Mathlib has this as\ncons_isCycle_iff.\n\n#check cons_isCycle_iff --  (cons h p).IsCycle ↔ p.IsPath ∧ s(u, v) ∉ p.edges\n\n\nIn fact the only way that the path p could already contain this edge is if p is\nthe walk consisting of the single edge h.symm. This gives the following variant of\ncons_isCycle_iff, which we will need later: cons h p is a cycle iff p is a path\nof length not equal to 1. (The simp only call in the proof uses tail_cons and\nisPath_copy, which involve Walk.copy: see Copy.)\n\ntheorem Walk.cons_isCycle_iff_length_ne_one {u v : V} (p : G.Walk v u)\n    (h : G.Adj u v) : (cons h p).IsCycle ↔ p.IsPath ∧ p.length ≠ 1 := by\n  have h0 : p.length ≠ 0 := fun h0 => h.ne (p.eq_of_length_eq_zero h0).symm\n  simp only [isCycle_iff_isPath_tail_and_le_length, getVert_cons_succ, tail_cons,\n    isPath_copy, length_cons, Nat.reduceLeDiff, ne_eq, and_congr_right_iff]\n  grind\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Trails, Paths, Circuits and Cycles","header":"8.4.3. Circuits and Cycles","id":"/Walks/#section-circuits-and-cycles"}});